Mini Quiz Show	2–20	Rounds, points, streaks, bonuses and a final
🏆 The one I'd build first

I'd make a "Party Arcade" containing all of these.

Instead of building 10 completely separate apps, you'd have:

HOME → CREATE ROOM → INVITE FRIENDS → CHOOSE GAME → PLAY → SCOREBOARD → NEXT GAME

That gives you something you can use repeatedly with friends.

And you could make the room itself persistent:

partyarcade.local/ABCD

Friends enter the room code on their phones, choose a nickname, and they're immediately in the lobby.

BRD: Party Arcade
Party Arcade — Business Requirements Document
Party Arcade
Business Requirements Document (BRD)

Version: 1.0
Product Type: Multiplayer web application / party-game platform
Primary Goal: Provide a collection of quick, competitive multiplayer games that friends can play together from their phones or computers.

1. Executive Summary

Party Arcade is a browser-based multiplayer gaming platform containing a collection of short, easy-to-learn party games.

Players can create a private room, invite friends using a room code or link, select a game, play several rounds, and accumulate points across games.

The platform is designed for casual leisure rather than long-form gaming.

A typical session should take approximately:

15–60 minutes

Players should be able to join without creating an account.

2. Problem Statement

When friends want to play something together, there is often friction:

Someone needs to install a game.
Different players may have different devices.
Players need accounts.
Some games take too long to learn.
Choosing what to play can take longer than actually playing.

Party Arcade should make multiplayer gaming as simple as:

Open → Join → Play

3. Product Vision

Create a personal digital arcade that friends can open anywhere and immediately start playing.

The experience should feel like having a box of party games available whenever people are together.

4. Target Users
Primary

Friends and families looking for casual multiplayer entertainment.

Secondary
Student groups
Office teams
Parties
Online friend groups
Couples
Small communities
5. Product Principles
Easy to Start

Players should understand the rules within seconds.

Short Sessions

Individual rounds should generally last between:

30 seconds and 5 minutes.

Everyone Participates

Games should minimize situations where players spend long periods waiting.

Device Friendly

Players should be able to use phones as controllers while a shared screen displays the game.

Competitive but Fun

Scores, rankings and leaderboards should create competition without making the experience overly serious.

6. Core User Journey
Host
Open Party Arcade.
Select Create Game Room.
Receive room code.
Share code/link with friends.
Players join.
Host selects a game.
Game begins.
Players compete.
Scores are updated.
Host selects another game.
Player
Open Party Arcade.
Enter room code.
Enter nickname.
Join lobby.
Wait for host.
Play.
Receive points.
View leaderboard.
7. Lobby Requirements

The lobby shall display:

Room code
Shareable invitation link
Player list
Player avatars/colors
Ready status
Game selection
Host controls

Host controls:

Start game
Select game
Remove player
Restart game
End room
8. Multiplayer Architecture

The system should support real-time synchronization.

The server should maintain authoritative game state including:

Current game
Current round
Players
Scores
Timers
Submitted answers
Winners
Game status

Clients should receive state updates in real time.

Potential technology:

WebSockets
WebRTC where appropriate
Real-time backend service
9. Game 1 — Typing Wars

Players receive the same text.

The objective is to type it as quickly and accurately as possible.

Metrics
WPM
Accuracy
Completion time
Correct characters
Incorrect characters
Backspaces
Modes
Normal
No Backspace
Blind Typing
Sudden Death
Winner

Winner can be determined using a combination of:

Speed + Accuracy

10. Game 2 — Word Blitz

Players receive a random set of letters.

Example:

A R T E N S

Players have 60 seconds to create as many valid words as possible.

Points depend on:

Word length
Word rarity
Speed

Duplicate words submitted by multiple players may receive reduced points.

11. Game 3 — Categories

The system generates:

Category: Animals
Letter: S

Players have 30 seconds.

Possible answers:

Snake
Shark
Sheep
Sparrow

Players receive points for valid unique answers.

12. Game 4 — Trivia Battle

All players receive the same question.

Example:

Which planet is closest to the Sun?

Players answer simultaneously.

Scoring:

Correct answer
Speed bonus
Streak bonus

The scoreboard updates after every question.

13. Game 5 — Emoji Pictionary

The system displays a sequence of emojis.

Example:

🎬 🦁 👑

Players guess:

The Lion King

First correct answer receives the highest score.

14. Game 6 — Quick Draw

The system provides one player with a secret drawing prompt.

Example:

"Elephant riding a bicycle"

The player draws.

Other players submit guesses.

Points are awarded for:

Correct guesses
Speed
Drawing completion
15. Game 7 — Reaction Race

Players wait for a visual signal.

Example:

WAIT... WAIT... WAIT... GO!

Players press a button as quickly as possible.

The system records reaction time in milliseconds.

False starts result in penalties.

Example:

Player 1 — 184 ms
Player 2 — 211 ms
Player 3 — 263 ms

16. Game 8 — Memory Battle

Players are shown a sequence.

Example:

🔴 🟢 🔵 🟡

The sequence disappears.

Players must reproduce it.

Each successful round increases the sequence length.

The last surviving player wins.

17. Game 9 — Imposter

Most players receive the same secret word.

One player receives:

IMPOSTER

Example:

Players:

Apple
Apple
Apple
IMPOSTER
Apple

Players discuss clues and vote.

The imposter wins if they survive the vote.

The group wins if they identify the imposter.

18. Game 10 — Number Duel

Players receive rapid mathematical challenges.

Example:

17 × 6 = ?

The first correct answer receives maximum points.

Difficulty increases throughout the game.

19. Universal Scoring System

Each game produces points.

Example:

1st: 100 points
